VAPT Assessment — IqraSec Phase 2

Project Overview

This project focuses on Vulnerability Assessment and Penetration Testing (VAPT) in an isolated cybersecurity lab using Kali Linux and Metasploitable 2.

The objective is to identify open ports, detect running services, review potential security weaknesses, and document findings with evidence.

Objectives

- Understand basic VAPT methodology.
- Set up a vulnerable virtual machine for safe practice.
- Perform port and service scanning using Nmap.
- Identify potential security vulnerabilities.
- Document verified findings and recommend remediation.

Tools Used

- Kali Linux — Security testing environment.
- Metasploitable 2 — Intentionally vulnerable practice machine.
- Nmap — Network and service scanning tool.
- Oracle VirtualBox — Virtual machine environment.

Lab Setup

The assessment is intended to run in an isolated virtual lab. Kali Linux and Metasploitable 2 should be connected to the same private lab network.

Important: Only scan systems you own or have explicit permission to test.

Methodology

1. Set up Kali Linux and Metasploitable 2 in VirtualBox.
2. Confirm that both virtual machines can communicate on the isolated network.
3. Identify the target machine's IP address.
4. Run an Nmap scan to identify open ports and services.
5. Review the scan results.
6. Document verified findings and suggested remediation.

Project Structure

IqraSec-Phase2-VAPT-Assessment/
├── README.md
├── recon.sh
├── report-template.md
└── evidence/
    └── scan-results.txt

The evidence files should be added only after the actual scan has been performed.

Evidence

The following evidence is planned for this project:

- scan-screenshot.png: Screenshot of the actual terminal showing the scan command and results.
- scan-results.txt: Text output generated by the scan script.

Actual scanning evidence and findings are currently pending verification.

Findings and Results

No scan results or vulnerabilities are claimed at this stage. Findings will be documented after running the scan and reviewing the actual output.

Remediation

Recommended remediation will depend on the verified findings. General recommendations include disabling unnecessary services, updating vulnerable software, restricting network access, and using secure configurations.

Conclusion

This project is intended to provide practical experience with basic vulnerability assessment, network reconnaissance, evidence collection, and security reporting.

Project Status: In Progress

